{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":1936534,"num_turns":160,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"88496f6c-666f-4604-8e17-c5db8c2b1772","total_cost_usd":19.870408499999996,"usage":{"input_tokens":287,"cache_creation_input_tokens":250577,"cache_read_input_tokens":28156627,"output_tokens":131326,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":250577,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1627,"cache_read_input_tokens":287407,"cache_creation_input_tokens":753,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":753},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":1660,"outputTokens":16,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.00174,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":287,"outputTokens":131326,"cacheReadInputTokens":28156627,"cacheCreationInputTokens":250577,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":19.868668499999995,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Handed off to review — `to-review`, checklist 16/16, revision `2026-07-28.r9`.\n\n## There is no verdict 08\n\nr8 was already at `to-review`. I re-ran it untouched first and it was **intact** (1331 checks, 164/164 negative gates, 44 board checks, all exit 0), so this run is a self-audit and everything below is something the audit found, not something a reviewer asked for.\n\n## Rule D1 — three declared consumers could run before this contract was accepted\n\nThe matrix has carried a prose `consumers` list since r1 and **it was never compared against the board's dependency graph**. Transitive closure over every task and bug record: **19 declared consumers, 16 reachable, 3 not** — `TASK-260715-1o9wjz`, `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`, `TASK-260715-29ws8l`. With `max_parallel=1` the scheduler was free to run the macOS credential resolver, the snapshot loader and the profile-trust contract while this matrix sat in `analysis` under its eighth changes-requested verdict.\n\nThis is the **verdict-05 F2 class one field over**: verdict 05 checked what a consumer *says*, A1/P1 check which *revision* it names, and nothing checked *when it runs*. A1/P1 only cover the two consumers carrying a revision label; for every other consumer the dependency edge is the sole ordering mechanism, and a missing edge fails silently because nothing reads a prose list. Four more obligation owners were absent from `consumers` entirely, three assigned from inside **prose fields** — which is why D1's derived set scans every string rather than three named lists.\n\n**Fix: one board edge.** `TASK-260715-29ws8l` blocked by this task orders all three, since it already blocks the other two — and it's semantically real, not a scheduling trick: its AC2 specifies \"App Group and `providerConfiguration` data\" and AC4 \"Keychain accessibility, access group\", exactly what G4 and K2 decide. Cycle-checked over the whole graph first. `done` is explicitly **not** an exemption basis; the one `upstream` exemption is verified, not accepted.\n\n## Rule N1 — the half of the count class r6 left open\n\n§9.1 records it biting already: r3 claimed a number over a list holding a different one, r4 corrected it by hand, r5 moved it again under K2. r6's scan covers App Group *record* counts only. N1 derives allowlist counts **and the rendered key list** from the rows in both artifacts, pins its exclusion lists, and states its bound instead of implying completeness.\n\n## Three defects in the new gates, caught by their own negative mutations\n\n- **R37's reverse check was tautological** — it counted the consumers list itself as a mention, so appending a consumer id present nowhere else *passed*.\n- A graph mutation's **premise was wrong, not the gate**: cutting `3jloqy`'s edge orphans nothing because `q5kjta` also blocks it. That's also the argument for a closure over an edge check.\n- A doc mutation didn't apply against a hard-wrapped sentence.\n\nThe fixed reverse check then found `TASK-260715-33oofa`: a declared consumer the contract never explained — its obligation sat in the *macOS* task's entry while 33oofa is the iOS half carrying the opposite instruction. Now recorded.\n\n## Gates — all exit 0\n\n| gate | result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `validate_matrix.py` (37 rules, R2–R38) | 2483 checks |\n| `mutate.py` | 202/202 (38 new; new **dependency-graph** harness) |\n| `check-portal-consumer.py` (A1, P1, **D1**) | 84 checks / 3 rules |\n| `preserve.py` r8→r9 | 122 assertions |\n| independent gate (no project harness) | r8 **false/exit 1** → r9 **true/exit 0** |\n| legacy guard, `task-board validate` | exit 0 |\n| `swift test` | 335 tests / 29 suites |\n\nNo entitlement decision, target row, profile row, record, assertion, assertion scope or C1 mutation set changed — preservation proves the only keys added are `consumerDependencyContract`/`numericClaimContract`. Board footprint: one edge added, plus `TASK-260728-q5kjta.scope` r8→r9 (**0-character delta**, required by `amendmentRule`). No portal mutation, no product source or spec touched.\n\nTwo things worth flagging: the board resource store **doesn't preserve the executable bit**, so `reviewer-gates.sh` must be run as `zsh <script>` (a `./` run exits 126 — I hit that during final verification); and this task still lists the now-`done` `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` in its own `Blocked By`, which is deliberate — D1's exemption register depends on that edge existing.","ttft_ms":6091,"ttft_stream_ms":5577,"time_to_request_ms":27,"type":"result","duration_ms":2035950,"uuid":"5d6d6e86-681c-469f-aa8f-1a7181d84c61"}
